{
  "abstract": "We give uniform finite approximation bounds for mechanical Collatz itineraries. If a binary Sturmian word $s$ has slope $\\beta=\\log_3 2$, then $|\\Phi(s)-y|_2\\ge2^{-14}y^{-2}$ for every positive integer $y$, uniformly over intercepts and boundary conventions. Every length-$N$ prefix therefore requires a positive integer source at least $2^{N/2-7}$. Every other irrational slope has an explicit finite exponent. This answers the displayed positive-integer approximation question in De Jesús's Sturmian and accelerated realizer manuscripts. The argument extends to Sturmian schedules of two halving counts, with exponent two at critical mean halving count, including binary words that are not Sturmian. A substitution corollary gives $N\\le64(D+1)^2+2(D+1)\\log_2 y$ when a prefix differs from a critical factor in at most $D$ positions. For positive reduced rationals with $q\\equiv1,3\\pmod8$, we obtain $|\\Phi(s)-p/q|_2\\ge2^{-14}H^{-2}(1+\\log_2H)^{-2}$, where $H=\\max(|p|,q)$. For every rational of odd denominator, of either sign, we also prove the weaker explicit bound $|\\Phi(s)-p/q|_2\\ge2^{-2401(2\\log_2H+18)^{143/10}}$. The proof combines known state-counting and drift with finite actual-state collisions. An observed full square makes the collision cycle balanced. Integer and restricted-denominator results exclude the cycle; the all-rational result retains it and imports Lagarias's quantitative lower bound for rational approximation to $\\log_3 2$ to limit the periodic match. For the characteristic intercept, a positive lattice basis from two standard words and Evertse's nondegenerate sum inequality improve the positive-integer exponent to $11/6+\\eta$ for every $\\eta>0$, with an ineffective constant. Seven fixed five-process audits support the proofs. Qualitative Sturmian irrationality and the cycle and Diophantine inputs are credited prior work. No polynomial irrationality measure for unrestricted rational approximants, general descent theorem, or solution of Collatz is claimed.",
